              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "To answer how much a mutual fund manager \"makes\" in terms of percentages, it is helpful to look at it from two different perspectives: **what percentage the mutual fund company charges investors** (the management fee) versus **what percentage the individual manager personally takes home** as compensation.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Fund\u2019s Percentage Fee (What the firm charges)\nWhen you invest in a mutual fund, the fund house charges an annual fee called an **expense ratio** to manage the money. The largest part of this is the **management fee**:\n\n* **Actively Managed Mutual Funds:** The management fee typically ranges from **0.5% to 1.5%** of Assets Under Management (AUM) annually. For example, if a manager oversees a $1 billion active fund with a 1.0% fee, the fund company collects $10 million in fees that year. \n* **Passively Managed Mutual Funds (Index Funds):** Because these funds simply track an index (like the S&P 500) and require less hands-on management, their fees are much lower, typically ranging from **0.02% to 0.2%** of AUM annually.\n* **No Performance Fees (Unlike Hedge Funds):** Unlike hedge fund managers who typically charge \"2 and 20\" (a 2% management fee **plus 20% of all profits**), mutual fund managers are legally prohibited in the U.S. from charging asymmetric performance fees. In rare cases, they may use a \"fulcrum fee,\" which symmetrically increases or decreases their fee percentage based on whether they beat or fall behind a benchmark index.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Individual Manager\u2019s Compensation (What the actual person makes)\nThe individual portfolio manager does *not* personally pocket the 0.5% to 1.5% fee. That money goes to the asset management company (e.g., Fidelity, Vanguard, BlackRock) to cover operating expenses, compliance, research analysts, marketing, and corporate profits. \n\nInstead, the individual mutual fund manager is a salaried employee whose compensation is structured around a base salary and a performance-based bonus:\n\n* **The Base Salary:** Typically ranges from **$200,000 to $450,000+** per year, depending on the size of the firm and the manager's experience.\n* **The Bonus (The \"Percentage\" part of their pay):** The bulk of a manager's income comes from their year-end bonus, which is often 1 to 3 times their base salary (and can be much higher for superstars). While there is no industry-wide fixed percentage, their bonus is calculated based on:\n    * **Performance against a benchmark (approx. 80% of funds):** Most managers have their bonuses tied to how well they beat their target benchmark (like the Russell 2000 or S&P 500) over a rolling 1-year, 3-year, and 5-year period. \n    * **Assets Under Management (approx. 20% of funds):** Some firms explicitly tie a portion of the bonus directly to a tiny percentage (usually measured in basis points, or hundredths of a percent) of the total AUM they manage or the net inflows of new investor cash they attract.\n\n### What does this equal in actual dollars?\nBecause of this structure, a mutual fund manager's total pay is highly dependent on the size of the fund they run:\n* **Median Fund Manager:** Earns around **$462,000** in total compensation (salary + bonus).\n* **Superstar/Large-Cap Fund Managers:** Managers overseeing mega-funds (tens of billions of dollars) frequently take home **$1 million to $5 million+** per year due to the massive scale of the fees their fund generates for the parent firm.",
